{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlstartstopcontinue"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"metadata":{"symbolKind":"enum","roleHeading":"Enumeration","role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"title":"MXLStartStopContinue","fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLStartStopContinue","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLStartStopContinue","kind":"identifier"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL20MXLStartStopContinueO"},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Enumeration-Cases","title":"Enumeration Cases","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLStartStopContinue\/continue","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLStartStopContinue\/start","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLStartStopContinue\/stop"],"generated":true},{"anchor":"Default-Implementations","title":"Default Implementations","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLStartStopContinue\/Equatable-Implementations"],"generated":true}],"kind":"symbol","sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL"]]},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLStartStopContinue"},"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To","type":"conformsTo","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SQ","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SH","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s16SendableMetatypeP"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLStartStopContinue","kind":"identifier"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The values of start, stop, and continue refer to how an element appears in musical score order, not in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"MusicXML document order. An element with a stop attribute may precede the corresponding element with a start"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"attribute within a MusicXML document. This is particularly common in multi-staff music. For example, the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"stopping point for a slur may appear in staff 1 before the starting point for the slur appears in staff 2"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"later in the document."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When multiple elements with the same tag are used within the same note, their order within the MusicXML"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"document should match the musical score order. For example, a note that marks both the end of one slur and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"start of a new slur should have the incoming slur element with a type of stop precede the outgoing slur"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"element with a type of start."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"The start-stop-continue type is used for an attribute of musical elements that can either start or stop, but","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"also need to refer to an intermediate point in the symbol, as for complex slurs or for formatting of symbols","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"across system breaks.","type":"text"}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"title":"Swift.SendableMetatype","type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s16SendableMetatypeP"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLStartStopContinue/Equatable-Implementations":{"type":"topic","role":"collectionGroup","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlstartstopcontinue\/equatable-implementations","kind":"article","title":"Equatable Implementations","identi